.Dd June 25, 2026
.Dt GETFACL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getfacl
.Nd display POSIX.1e access control lists
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl ad
.Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility writes to standard output the POSIX.1e access control list (ACL)
of each
.Ar file
operand.
.Pp
For every file, a three-line header is printed giving the file name, the
name (or numeric id) of its owner, and the name (or numeric id) of its
owning group, followed by the entries of the access ACL, one per line, in
the canonical
.Xr acl 3
text form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# file: foo
# owner: alice
# group: users
user::rw-
user:bob:rw-
group::r--
mask::rw-
other::r--
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Li user:: ,
.Li group::
and
.Li other::
entries correspond to the owner, owning-group and other permission bits of
the file mode.
A
.Li user:
or
.Li group:
entry that names a qualifier grants permissions to an additional user or
group; the
.Li mask::
entry is the upper bound on the permissions any named entry and the owning
group may be granted.
A file that has no extended ACL is reported as the trivial ACL implied by
its mode bits.
.Pp
For a directory, its default ACL (the ACL inherited by files and
subdirectories created within it; see
.Xr setfacl 1 )
is printed after the access ACL with each line prefixed by
.Li default: .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Display only the access ACL.
.It Fl d
Display only the default ACL.
.El
.Pp
The
.Fl a
and
.Fl d
options are mutually meaningful only for directories; an ordinary file has
no default ACL.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
On MINIX 3, ACLs are stored as extended attributes (see
.Xr extattr 3 )
in the system namespace and are supported by the MFS file system when it is
created with the version 4 on-disk format
.Pq Nm mkfs.mfs Fl 4 .
ACLs are recorded in the portable
.Dv POSIX_ACL_XATTR
binary representation.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Show the ACL of the current directory:
.Pp
.Dl $ getfacl .
.Pp
Show only the default ACL of a directory:
.Pp
.Dl $ getfacl -d /shared
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setfacl 1 ,
.Xr acl 3 ,
.Xr extattr 3
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility implements the access control list interface described in the
withdrawn IEEE 1003.1e draft 17
.Pq Dq POSIX.1e ,
as also provided by
.Fx
and Linux.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
utility appeared in MINIX 3 in 2026.
